def clamp(value: float, min_val: float, max_val: float) -> float:
    if min_val > max_val:
        # Range is invalid; as per test, return value unchanged
        return value
    if value < min_val:
        return min_val
    if value > max_val:
        return max_val
    return value
```

In particular, note that when `min_val > max_val`, we simply return `value` without clamping — this matches the explicit test case expectations. The rest of the logic clamps normally within `[min_val, max_val]`. I didn't need special handling for `min_val == max_val` because the normal comparisons make it consistent: if `value < min_val == max_val`, returns `min_val`; if `value > max_val == min_val`, returns `max_val`; else returns value, which can only be equal to that single value.

